\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{CIVO mechanism.}
The RSSM develops a $2.78\times$ reward prediction gap under trained conditioning (Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional}. Gradient magnitude $\neq$ causal importance: the reward head (38--49\% of $\nabla_\theta e_{\dt}$) fails when blocked alone; the observation decoder (16--22\%) carries the critical signal; full-pathway SGS (0/3) confirms forward-pass conditioning as the irreducible vulnerability. CIVO is upstream of estimation error---dual critics converge (\S\ref{sec:arch_results})---so standard corrections are inapplicable; MPC re-planning prevents accumulation (Appendix~\ref{sec:mpc_immune}).

\paragraph{Practitioner guidelines.}
Monitor via NVE diagnostic ($\geq 1.5\times$, AUROC${=}0.953$; Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}). Freezing the $\dt$-embedding converts sustained CIVO (6/6 trained locomotion, NVE $1.53$--$108\times$) to transient spikes that self-correct within 20K steps (2/3 frozen Walker, $1.71$--$1.88\times$; 0/2 frozen Cheetah); untreated, extreme CIVO ($70.6\times$) causes irrecoverable damage (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_analysis}).

\paragraph{Implications for robot learning.}
No $\dt$-embedding-dependent CIVO was observed in manipulation: all three ManiSkill environments exhibit ve-ratio transients (peaks $4$--$11\times$), but frozen controls show comparable or higher peaks (Table~\ref{tab:cross_domain}), establishing environment-specific origin. A possible mediator is reward density: locomotion provides continuous rewards accumulating coherent $\dt$-embedding bias, while manipulation rewards are sparse and contact-gated.

\subsection{Cross-Environment TCN Evaluation}
\label{sec:tcn_cross_env}

Table~\ref{tab:tcn_full} reports the full TCN evaluation across environments and noise magnitudes.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{TCN results across environments and noise magnitudes. Prevention = peak NVE $< 1.5\times$ (35K+).}
\label{tab:tcn_full}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Env} & $\sigma$ & \textbf{Seed} & \textbf{NVE$_{\max}$} & \textbf{Prevented?} & \textbf{Eval (\%)} \\
\midrule
Walker & 0.1 & s0 & 1.02 & \checkmark$^*$ & 55\% \\
Walker & 0.2 & s0 & 0.91 & \checkmark & 91\% \\
Walker & 0.2 & s1 & 0.64 & \checkmark & 45\% \\
Walker & 0.2 & s2 & 2.11$^\dagger$ & $\times$ & ${\sim}5\%$ \\
\rowcolor{gray!15}
Walker & 0.2 & s3 & 3.04$^\P$ & $\times$ & 11\% \\
Walker & 0.5 & s0 & 3.97 & $\times$ & 27\% \\
Walker & 0.5 & s1 & 1.64 & $\times$ & 41\% \\
\midrule
Cheetah & 0.2 & s0 & 1.90 & $\times$ & crashed \\
Cheetah & 0.5 & s0 & 10.67 & $\times$ & 26\% \\
Cheetah & 1.0 & s0 & $\leq$0.30 & \checkmark$^\S$ & 15\% \\
\midrule
PickCube & 0.2 & s0 & 3.71 & $\times$ & failing \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{1pt}
{\raggedright\footnotesize $^*$Borderline: NVE${=}1.02\times$ at 95K, below $1.5\times$ threshold but above $1.0\times$.\\
$^\dagger$NVE $= 2.11\times$ at 95K, still rising; eval ${\sim}5\%$ of baseline.\\
$^\P$Late-onset CIVO: NVE declined to $0.67\times$ at 95K before phase-transition-like spike to $3.04\times$ at 105K; eval 349.6 at 95K $\to$ 71.8 at 105K $\to$ 104.5 at 215K.\\
$^\S$CIVO prevented (NVE${\leq}0.30$); peak eval 116\% baseline at 35K, declining to 15\% by 215K via progressive policy erosion. See ``impossible dose'' discussion.\par}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Cheetah Run $\sigma{=}0.5$: transient spike with permanent eval damage.}
Cheetah $\sigma{=}0.5$ exhibits a novel failure mode: a transient CIVO spike (NVE $= 10.67\times$ at 55K) followed by NVE recovery ($< 0.3\times$ by 95K), but permanent evaluation damage (26\% of baseline). The value signal self-corrects while the policy does not recover, suggesting that CIVO-induced representational damage during the spike is irreversible even when the critic subsequently recalibrates. This trajectory parallels the obs-decoder SGS seed~3 pattern (\S\ref{sec:seed_divergence}), where partial policy recovery coexists with persistent critic damage.

\paragraph{Cheetah Run $\sigma{=}0.2$: insufficient dose.}
At $\sigma{=}0.2$, Cheetah reaches NVE $= 1.9\times$ at 55K---exceeding the $1.5\times$ threshold---consistent with Cheetah's higher baseline CIVO severity ($8$--$108\times$ vs.\ Walker's $2$--$6\times$). The noise magnitude that suffices for Walker is inadequate for Cheetah, but increasing $\sigma$ to 0.5 trades CIVO prevention for a different failure mode rather than achieving mitigation.

\paragraph{PickCube-v1 $\sigma{=}0.2$: sparse-reward failure.}
PickCube reaches NVE $= 3.71\times$ at 52.5K with $\sigma{=}0.2$, indicating that TCN provides no protection in sparse-reward manipulation tasks. The combination of sparse rewards and temporal conditioning may amplify the fidelity gap beyond what representation-level noise can attenuate.

\paragraph{Dose-response analysis.}
We observe $\sigma{=}0.2$ as a local optimum on Walker Walk, achieving complete CIVO suppression in 2 of 4 seeds. Seeds~2 and~3 both fail but via distinct CIVO onset dynamics: seed~2 shows gradual escalation (NVE $= 2.11\times$ at 95K, still rising, eval ${\sim}5\%$), while seed~3 exhibits a phase-transition-like onset---NVE declined to $0.67\times$ at 95K before spiking to $3.04\times$ at 105K (eval 349.6 $\to$ 71.8 in 10K steps). When TCN fails to prevent CIVO onset, the resulting trajectory qualitatively resembles untreated CIVO in its terminal outcome---runaway NVE and eval collapse. The two failure modes (gradual escalation vs.\ sudden onset after apparent suppression) reinforce that TCN operates as a binary gate on CIVO onset rather than providing graded attenuation. Below the optimum, we observe $\sigma{=}0.1$ as insufficient (NVE $= 1.02\times$---borderline, eval 55\%); above it, $\sigma{=}0.5$ overdoses (oscillatory CIVO, eval $\leq 41\%$). Cross-seed variance at $\sigma{=}0.2$ (eval 5--91\%) exceeds the cross-dose difference by a wide margin, reinforcing that seed effects dominate dose effects. TCN is the only tested intervention achieving complete CIVO suppression in any configuration, but its high seed sensitivity and failure to transfer across environments position it as a \emph{diagnostic probe}---identifying the representation level as the relevant intervention target---rather than a deployable mitigation.

\paragraph{Cheetah Run ``impossible dose'' dilemma.}
Exp\_030c (224K steps) reveals a qualitatively different dose-response on Cheetah. At $\sigma{=}1.0$, CIVO is eliminated (NVE${\leq}0.30$ throughout) and initial learning is \emph{unimpaired}: peak eval reaches 372 at 35K (116\% of baseline), demonstrating that $\sigma{=}1.0$ noise does not prevent policy acquisition. However, cumulative actor gradient interference produces progressive policy erosion---eval declines from 372 to 47 by 215K (15\% of baseline)---while the critic remains well-calibrated (value\_mean${=}12.4$, NVE${=}0.26$ at 215K). This establishes that \emph{policy degradation $\neq$ value corruption}: accurate value signals are insufficient when the actor cannot optimize effectively in noise-perturbed imagination.

This ``correct values, failed policy'' pattern provides indirect evidence that CIVO is mechanistically distinct from standard overestimation. Double Q-learning and ensemble methods address critic estimation error, but at $\sigma{=}1.0$ the critic is accurate and the policy still collapses---a failure mode orthogonal to what these countermeasures target. The impossible dose dilemma scales with environment complexity: Cheetah's baseline CIVO severity ($8$--$108\times$ NVE) requires $\sigma \geq 1.0$ to suppress, but this exceeds the actor's gradient signal-to-noise tolerance. On Walker, a narrow effective window exists around $\sigma{=}0.2$; on Cheetah, the effective dose ($\sigma \geq 1.0$) exceeds the tolerable dose, and environments with higher CIVO severity will systematically lack a viable dose window.
